-- title:
--   Ring as fibre product of two quotients with zero intersection
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a commutative ring and let $I_1, I_2$ be ideals of $A$ whose intersection (the lattice meet in the ideal lattice) is the zero ideal, $I_1 \sqcap I_2 = \bot$. The assertion is a conjunction of two statements. First, the map $A \to A/I_1 \times A/I_2$ sending $a$ to the pair of its residue classes $(a \bmod I_1, a \bmod I_2)$ is injective. Second, for all $x_1 \in A/I_1$ and $x_2 \in A/I_2$ whose images in $A/(I_1 + I_2)$ agree — the images being taken along the canonical maps induced by the inclusions $I_1 \le I_1 \sqcup I_2$ and $I_2 \le I_1 \sqcup I_2$, where $I_1 \sqcup I_2$ is the ideal generated by $I_1$ and $I_2$ — there exists $a \in A$ with $a \bmod I_1 = x_1$ and $a \bmod I_2 = x_2$. Together these say that $A \to A/I_1 \times_{A/(I_1+I_2)} A/I_2$ is bijective onto the fibre product, phrased without introducing a fibre-product type; the hypothesis $I_1 \sqcap I_2 = \bot$ enters only in the injectivity half, the second half being proved for arbitrary ideals.
--
--   This is the standard identification $A \cong A/I_1 \times_{A/(I_1+I_2)} A/I_2$ for ideals with zero intersection, the ring-theoretic form of gluing a scheme along two closed subschemes. It is used in establishing that a scheme covered by two affine closed subschemes whose underlying closed sets cover it is affine, in [`AlgebraicGeometry.isAffine_of_isClosedImmersion_of_isAffine_of_range_union_range`](thm.html#AlgebraicGeometry.isAffine_of_isClosedImmersion_of_isAffine_of_range_union_range).
